ent":"`heights` is a grid of cell heights. You start at the top-left cell and want to reach the bottom-right cell, moving up, down, left or right. The effort of a route is the largest absolute height difference between two consecutive cells on it.\n\nReturn the minimum effort needed.","constraints":["1 <= heights.length <= 100","1 <= heights[0].length <= 100","1 <= heights[i][j] <= 1e6"],"hints":["A route's cost is a maximum, not a sum, but Dijkstra still works: the cost of a route never decreases as it gets longer.","Run Dijkstra where the cost to reach a neighbour is max(current effort, |height difference|).","Binary search on the effort, checking reachability with BFS, is another way; so is union-find adding edges in order of difference."],"explain":"The route 1 → 3 → 5 → 3 → 5 (down the left column, then along the bottom row) never climbs or drops more than 2 between neighbouring cells. Every other route has a step of at least 2 as well, so the answer is 2."},
